The underfit model (k=5) misses the high-frequency component entirely, while k=20 captures both
sine components. The `check()` method correctly flagged the k=5 model.

## Practical diagnostic workflow

A good diagnostic workflow after fitting any GAM:

1. **`model.summary()`**: check that EDF values make sense, deviance explained is reasonable
2. **`model.check()`**: verify basis dimensions are adequate (k-index test)
3. **Residuals vs. fitted**: check for patterns indicating wrong family or missing terms
4. **QQ plot**: check the distributional assumption
5. **If any diagnostic fails**: consider increasing `k`, changing the family, adding terms, or
   restructuring the model

::: {.callout-tip}
## The most common fix

The most common diagnostic issue is an inadequate basis dimension. REML will never overfit even with
a generous `k`, so it is always safe to increase `k`. Start with the default (10), check, and
double if the k-index test is significant. Repeat until the test passes.
:::
